1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,162
WWW.MY-SUBS.CO

﻿1
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:08,980
(BELL RINGS)

2
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:44,460
Who will groan first when
they see it, Granny or Papa?

3
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:49,020
(CHUCKLES) I should think they'll
howl at the moon in unison.

4
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,500
What in God's name is this?

5
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:10,140
Well I never! Where did this come from?

6
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:14,180
I ordered it in London,
picked it up on the way back.

7
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:16,500
It's an AC. Well, at least it's English.

8
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:18,460
Welcome back, my darling.

9
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:23,260
How was the honeymoon? My eyes
have been opened. Don't I know it!

10
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:25,340
Anna get back all right? Yes.

11
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:32,220
Who's that for? Why are
you doing it down here?

12
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:35,580
It's for Mr Matthew. It
was creased, I'm ironing it.

13
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:39,420
You're never looking after
him? What about Mr Molesley?

14
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:42,900
He's staying at Crawley
House. Why wasn't I asked?

15
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:47,140
Mr Carson thought it best. Did
he? I wonder how that came about?

16
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:51,380
If you're learning your job,
never open a shirt in a room

17
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:55,340
where it might be marked,
let alone put studs in it.

18
00:01:55,340 --> 00:01:59,180
Do that in a dressing room
and nowhere else. Thank you.

19
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:02,860
Yes, you're always trying
to be so very helpful.

20
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:09,940
How did you enjoy the south
of France? It was lovely,

21
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:11,980
but almost too hot, even now.

22
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:16,540
It's a shame they close things
in the summer. I love the sun.

23
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:21,060
So we can see. You couldn't be in
Cannes in summer, it's unbearable.

24
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:24,340
I could. Just how long is she here for?

25
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:27,220
Who knows? No guest should be admitted

26
00:02:27,220 --> 00:02:29,940
with no departure date settled.

27
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:32,140
You won't get any argument from me.

28
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:36,180
There's a hideous pile of post.
I've put it on the hall table.

29
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:39,220
Don't look at it tonight.
What have you been up to?

30
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:42,540
I've found myself a new occupation,

31
00:02:42,540 --> 00:02:45,100
but Violet doesn't think it's appropriate.

32
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:47,220
Can we talk about it afterwards?

33
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:52,260
Are there still forbidden subjects
in 1920? I can't believe this.

34
00:02:53,420 --> 00:02:55,660
I speak of taste rather than law.

35
00:02:55,660 --> 00:02:58,300
Well, it's not my taste.

36
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:01,740
What about you, Cora? I agree with Mama.

37
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:05,180
Some subjects are not
suitable for every ear.

38
00:03:05,180 --> 00:03:08,380
Oh, pas devant les domestiques.

39
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:11,020
Come on, my dear, Carson and Alfred

40
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:13,780
know more about life than we ever will.

41
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:19,500
Can't we stop this? How?
She's like a runaway train.

44
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:30,540
Why do you call him poor
or old? He's neither.

45
00:03:31,740 --> 00:03:35,820
Isn't it dangerous to let this
Strallan nonsense simmer on?

46
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:40,060
To be fair, I don't think it's
coming from him. Ask him to end it.

47
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:42,980
He'll be more effective than if we try.

48
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:47,180
Ooh, she ate it, then?

49
00:03:47,180 --> 00:03:49,460
Never sure about Americans and offal.

50
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:52,940
She'll eat whatever you put
in front of her. What a gob.

51
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:55,700
Mr Carson was going to
put a bag over her head.

52
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:59,180
Oops. Mrs Levinson
knows you make fun of her

53
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:03,980
because she makes fun of
you. We're all square, then.

54
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:07,020
It's not drying properly,
the oven's not hot enough.

55
00:04:07,020 --> 00:04:09,540
A bad workman always blames his tools.

56
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:14,860
Are you busy? No. We're eating
in half an hour but it's all done.

57
00:04:14,860 --> 00:04:16,940
Well, if you could spare a minute.

58
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:23,860
This is very good, you didn't
open it for me. Certainly I did.

59
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:26,340
To welcome you into this house as my son.

60
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:29,220
I can't tell you how glad it makes me.

61
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:36,620
I want us always to feel we
can be honest with each other.

62
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:42,340
Of course. Because Mary's told me
about your present difficulties.

63
00:04:44,180 --> 00:04:46,460
Oh, she was right,

64
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:50,300
losing Downton will affect
you both more than anyone.

65
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:55,940
Has she told you about the
will of Lavinia's father?

66
00:04:56,220 --> 00:04:59,500
Yes, your father told me all about it,

67
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:04,460
but I cannot understand why so
much money was put into one company.

68
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:08,780
I couldn't agree more. Now we're
to be turned out of Downton.

69
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:12,020
Even Lloyd George can't want that.

70
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:14,180
I'm not sure he's a good example.

71
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:18,100
The point is have we overlooked something,

72
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:22,380
some source of revenue
previously untapped?

73
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:28,460
If only we had some
coal, or gravel, or tin.

74
00:05:28,460 --> 00:05:32,140
I can think of someone
who's got plenty of tin.

75
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:35,580
So you help women who have... fallen over?

76
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:40,140
Not quite. Isabelle helps women
who have to degrade themselves

77
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:42,820
to survive. There's a centre in York.

78
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:45,980
No addresses, please.
Alfred will make notes.

79
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:51,700
What do you do for these women?
First, we like to send them away...

80
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:54,700
to rest. I should think they need it.

81
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:58,660
And then we try to find
them alternative employment.

82
00:05:58,660 --> 00:06:02,860
The War destroyed many households,
thousands of bread winners died.

83
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:05,300
So you want me to contribute?

84
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:10,060
You don't have to give money
after every conversation. No?

85
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:13,940
Isn't that what the English
expect of rich Americans?

86
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:19,940
Why can't you benefit from the
will? You've done nothing wrong.

87
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:23,380
He didn't know I'd broken
his daughter's heart.

88
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:25,620
It was to reward my fidelity

89
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:27,660
when in fact I'd betrayed her.

90
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:31,900
If I kept that money, I'd be no
better than a common criminal.

91
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:34,540
I see.

92
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:37,980
If that's how you feel,
there's no more to be said.

93
00:06:39,540 --> 00:06:43,300
I'm ever so sorry, my lord, I
thought you were out of here.

94
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:45,420
We should be. Please. We're going now.

95
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:54,540
It's a lump, there's no
point dithering about that.

96
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:59,020
What are you going to do
about it? I don't know.

97
00:06:59,020 --> 00:07:04,820
Tomorrow, you'll make an appointment
with the doctor and see what he says.

98
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:10,060
But what if it's... If it
is, and I'm not saying it is,

99
00:07:10,060 --> 00:07:11,860
it's best to know now.

100
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:15,380
I suppose so.

101
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:21,180
Now, look, you'll not be alone for
a minute if you don't want to be,

102
00:07:21,980 --> 00:07:24,620
but we have to get it seen to.

103
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:27,740
Then there's expense.

104
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:32,940
If you must pay money, better
to a doctor than an undertaker.

105
00:07:36,180 --> 00:07:40,060
If that's an example of your
bedside manner, Mrs Patmore,

106
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:42,580
I think I'd sooner face it alone.

107
00:07:47,940 --> 00:07:51,540
It seems shocking for Anna
to find me en deshabille.

108
00:07:51,540 --> 00:07:55,580
I'm made of stout stuff,
sir, don't worry about that.

109
00:07:59,580 --> 00:08:03,300
Are you seeing Bates today?
I am, and I can't wait.

110
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:05,420
Give him our best wishes.

111
00:08:05,860 --> 00:08:08,980
It still seems odd to
be found in your bed.

112
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:13,820
But very nice. Oh, as nice as nice can be.

113
00:08:19,340 --> 00:08:21,540
I'm going to see Jarvis today

114
00:08:21,540 --> 00:08:25,100
and find out what houses are
available. Do you have to?

115
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:29,460
These are our last days here. I
thought something might've turned up

116
00:08:29,460 --> 00:08:32,020
while we were away but it seems it hasn't.

117
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:36,220
Would you rather wait till we have
to go and find a new house then?

118
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:40,540
After all, darling, you're
the one who's pushing us out.

119
00:09:01,357 --> 00:09:03,357
(MAN) Not for me, thank you.

120
00:09:13,117 --> 00:09:16,517
But Mary's only just
got back from honeymoon.

121
00:09:16,517 --> 00:09:19,717
It's her family time. But you are...

122
00:09:19,717 --> 00:09:22,877
Stop saying I'm family when I'm not.

123
00:09:23,557 --> 00:09:26,877
I'll be there for the
big dinner next week.

124
00:09:27,397 --> 00:09:31,517
What is it? I know you
don't mean to hurt me, but...

125
00:09:31,517 --> 00:09:35,437
Of course I don't. That's
the last thing I'd wish to do.

126
00:09:35,437 --> 00:09:39,957
Then why do you shove me away? I
don't want to, not at all, but...

127
00:09:39,957 --> 00:09:43,997
If you're going to talk about your
wretched arm again, I won't listen.

128
00:09:43,997 --> 00:09:47,557
It's not just my arm. I'm too old for you.

129
00:09:47,557 --> 00:09:51,197
You need a young chap
with his life ahead of him.

130
00:09:51,197 --> 00:09:55,677
Your life's ahead of you. If you
knew how much I want to believe that.

131
00:09:55,677 --> 00:09:57,637
Then it's settled.

132
00:09:57,637 --> 00:10:00,117
You're not going to push me away any more

133
00:10:00,117 --> 00:10:05,237
and you are coming for dinner
tonight. That's all there is to it.

134
00:10:13,597 --> 00:10:16,957
How did you get on with Vera's book?

135
00:10:16,957 --> 00:10:20,277
I had a few answers waiting
for me when I got back

136
00:10:20,277 --> 00:10:22,477
and two returned "address unknown".

137
00:10:22,477 --> 00:10:26,557
Who from? Let me see, one
was a Mr Harlip, I think,

138
00:10:26,557 --> 00:10:29,877
and the other was Mrs Bartlett.

139
00:10:30,597 --> 00:10:35,157
He doesn't matter, he was a cousin
in the north, she never saw him,

140
00:10:35,157 --> 00:10:37,477
but Mrs Bartlett's a shame.

141
00:10:37,477 --> 00:10:41,637
She lived on the corner, she
was very friendly. I'll find her.

142
00:10:42,917 --> 00:10:44,997
Tell me about France.

143
00:10:44,997 --> 00:10:47,717
Did you eat frog's legs
and dance the can-can?

144
00:10:48,717 --> 00:10:52,237
No, but I bought a garter.

145
00:10:57,837 --> 00:10:59,957
You've had no other symptoms?

146
00:11:00,357 --> 00:11:02,517
Not that I'm aware of.

147
00:11:02,517 --> 00:11:05,037
You're not feeling ill or tired?

148
00:11:05,037 --> 00:11:07,997
I can't swear to not feeling tired

149
00:11:07,997 --> 00:11:10,197
but nothing out of the ordinary.

150
00:11:10,197 --> 00:11:12,237
Very well.

151
00:11:12,237 --> 00:11:15,677
I'm going to conduct a
preliminary examination.

152
00:11:15,677 --> 00:11:17,557
Do you mind if I stay?

153
00:11:18,357 --> 00:11:20,397
I should prefer it.

154
00:11:24,317 --> 00:11:27,437
What have you got there?
Mr Matthew's tailcoat.

155
00:11:27,437 --> 00:11:30,357
What do you think that is? Hard to say.

156
00:11:30,357 --> 00:11:32,917
I've tried everything
but I can't shift it.

157
00:11:32,917 --> 00:11:37,117
I'll give you a tip, if you
like. Would you, really? Yeah.

158
00:11:37,117 --> 00:11:41,277
Keep it to yourself, don't want
to give away all my secrets.

159
00:11:44,917 --> 00:11:48,637
Oh, there you are, my
dear. Good morning, Granny.

160
00:11:49,037 --> 00:11:51,117
I've been looking for you.

161
00:11:51,117 --> 00:11:54,797
Now I want to know if we're serious,

162
00:11:55,237 --> 00:11:59,277
about getting that woman, about
asking your other grandmother

163
00:11:59,277 --> 00:12:01,557
to come to our aid. She's made of money

164
00:12:01,557 --> 00:12:05,837
and there's only Mama and Uncle
Harold to share it when she's gone.

165
00:12:05,837 --> 00:12:09,117
We can't wait that
long, she'll bury us all.

166
00:12:09,117 --> 00:12:11,597
No, we must act now.

167
00:12:11,597 --> 00:12:16,757
We must make her feel it
is her duty to save Downton.

168
00:12:16,757 --> 00:12:20,917
But how? What can we do?
Get her to sense its value,

169
00:12:21,517 --> 00:12:24,317
its vital role in the area.

170
00:12:24,317 --> 00:12:26,477
You're her granddaughter.

171
00:12:26,477 --> 00:12:29,477
This will be your house if it survives.

172
00:12:29,477 --> 00:12:33,797
Surely you can make something of
that if she has a heart at all.

173
00:12:33,797 --> 00:12:36,277
We'll come for tea this afternoon.

174
00:12:36,277 --> 00:12:38,517
Then we can begin.

175
00:12:39,757 --> 00:12:43,837
There are several stages to go
through before a cause for despair.

176
00:12:43,837 --> 00:12:47,997
What stages? When you
come back in a day or two,

177
00:12:47,997 --> 00:12:50,077
I'll remove fluid from the cyst.

178
00:12:50,077 --> 00:12:53,357
With luck it'll be clear
and that will be that.

179
00:12:53,357 --> 00:12:56,197
How will you do it? With a syringe.

180
00:12:56,197 --> 00:13:00,797
Will it hurt? Since he has to
do it, whether it hurts or not,

181
00:13:00,797 --> 00:13:03,277
there's no point to that question.

182
00:13:03,277 --> 00:13:07,757
What I want to know is what
happens if the fluid is not clear?

183
00:13:07,757 --> 00:13:12,677
It'll be sent away for analysis.
Because it may be... cancer?

184
00:13:14,717 --> 00:13:18,477
It may be cancer but I'm
fairly certain it is not.

185
00:13:18,477 --> 00:13:23,717
There you are. It's very, very
unlikely, isn't it, Doctor?

186
00:13:25,037 --> 00:13:27,677
If the doctor treats me like an adult,

187
00:13:27,677 --> 00:13:30,157
why do insist on treating me like a child?

188
00:13:34,117 --> 00:13:37,317
Does this seem slow to you? Not really.

189
00:13:39,717 --> 00:13:43,637
Mrs Levinson's going to the Dower
House with the others for tea.

190
00:13:46,277 --> 00:13:48,317
I think he likes me.

191
00:13:48,637 --> 00:13:50,917
He's being friendly, that's all.

192
00:13:58,637 --> 00:14:02,997
Are you all right? You seem to
have been slaving away for hours.

193
00:14:02,997 --> 00:14:06,997
I want to be up to date before
I get back to the office.

194
00:14:06,997 --> 00:14:09,837
Anything from Mr Swire's lawyer?

195
00:14:12,677 --> 00:14:15,157
You can read it if you like.

196
00:14:16,837 --> 00:14:20,917
(SIGHS) So you are
definitely Reggie's heir?

197
00:14:20,917 --> 00:14:24,877
Looks like it, but if they
have to get a death certificate

198
00:14:24,877 --> 00:14:29,677
out of the Indian authorities, it
won't be settled by Tuesday. Good.

199
00:14:29,677 --> 00:14:33,917
Why is it good? The delay may
give you time to change your mind.

200
00:14:33,917 --> 00:14:36,677
Stop punishing me, Mary, please.

201
00:14:36,677 --> 00:14:40,717
If I accepted the legacy, I'd
take money under false pretences.

202
00:14:40,717 --> 00:14:44,797
I'd be stealing. Your father
understands now why can't you?

203
00:14:44,797 --> 00:14:48,437
He doesn't understand, he
just doesn't want to beg.

204
00:14:48,437 --> 00:14:51,877
Anyway, I'm off to Granny's
for tea. See you later.

205
00:14:54,677 --> 00:14:56,957
I do love you so terribly much.

206
00:14:56,957 --> 00:14:59,757
Yes, I know you do.

207
00:15:08,837 --> 00:15:11,397
So what's Harold doing now?

208
00:15:11,397 --> 00:15:14,277
His idee fixe is yachts.

209
00:15:14,277 --> 00:15:16,437
Bigger yachts, faster yachts.

210
00:15:16,437 --> 00:15:18,797
Something with yachts.

211
00:15:18,797 --> 00:15:22,557
Is he happy? He's much
too busy to find out.

212
00:15:22,557 --> 00:15:27,157
It always seems so strange to
me that Cora has a brother. Why?

213
00:15:27,157 --> 00:15:31,357
If there's a boy, the
daughters don't get anything.

214
00:15:31,357 --> 00:15:35,397
There's no such thing as an
English heiress with a brother.

215
00:15:35,397 --> 00:15:37,517
Why do we never see him?

216
00:15:37,517 --> 00:15:40,037
Harold hates to leave America.

217
00:15:40,037 --> 00:15:43,277
Curious. He hates to leave America.

218
00:15:43,277 --> 00:15:45,637
I should hate to go there.

219
00:15:46,237 --> 00:15:48,517
You don't mean that, Granny,

220
00:15:48,517 --> 00:15:50,797
when we're both so drawn to America.

221
00:15:51,317 --> 00:15:54,997
Indeed, indeed we are.
Never more than now,

222
00:15:55,597 --> 00:15:59,637
when the bond between the Crawleys
and Levinsons is so strong.

223
00:15:59,637 --> 00:16:02,117
That's nice, if you mean it, Mama.

224
00:16:02,637 --> 00:16:04,397
I do.

225
00:16:04,397 --> 00:16:08,837
It is marvellous the way our
families support each other.

226
00:16:08,837 --> 00:16:14,197
You mean you needed the Levinson
cash to keep the Crawleys on top?

227
00:16:14,197 --> 00:16:16,477
I'm not sure we'd put it that way.

228
00:16:16,477 --> 00:16:20,997
I'm quite sure we would not. But
I hope you feel Mama's fortune

229
00:16:20,997 --> 00:16:25,757
has been well spent in shoring
up an ancient family. Hm.

230
00:16:26,597 --> 00:16:29,157
You gotta spend it on something!

231
00:16:35,557 --> 00:16:38,037
What happened here? I...

232
00:16:38,597 --> 00:16:40,477
I just...

233
00:16:40,477 --> 00:16:44,357
You just what? There was a
mark. I know there was a mark,

234
00:16:44,357 --> 00:16:48,517
but you didn't need to burn
it away. What have you done?

235
00:16:48,517 --> 00:16:52,797
I'll go down in my dinner jacket,
send it to my tailor in the morning.

236
00:16:52,797 --> 00:16:56,477
Come on, nobody's died,
just find the dinner jacket.

237
00:16:59,037 --> 00:17:03,397
There don't seem to be any
glasses for the pudding wine.

238
00:17:03,397 --> 00:17:06,757
Are they having one
tonight? It's on the menus.

239
00:17:06,757 --> 00:17:10,397
I don't write them for my
own amusement. I dare say not.

240
00:17:10,397 --> 00:17:13,357
I am trying, and so far failing,

241
00:17:13,357 --> 00:17:17,597
to persuade his lordship to bring
the staff levels back up to snuff,

242
00:17:17,597 --> 00:17:21,597
but until he does it is vital
that you pull your weight.

243
00:17:24,677 --> 00:17:26,717
(SIGHS)

244
00:17:26,717 --> 00:17:29,437
Newport's not a jungle, not at all,

245
00:17:29,437 --> 00:17:32,317
but it is a little less formal.

246
00:17:32,317 --> 00:17:36,597
Matthew obviously wants you to
feel at home in his play clothes.

247
00:17:36,597 --> 00:17:40,117
Don't blame me. Alfred and
I had a disaster earlier.

248
00:17:40,117 --> 00:17:43,917
What happened? He managed
to burn a hole in my tails.

249
00:17:43,917 --> 00:17:46,797
Don't worry, it can be
mended. Careful, Carson.

250
00:17:46,797 --> 00:17:49,077
I beg your pardon, my lord.

251
00:17:49,077 --> 00:17:53,517
I like dinner jackets, sometimes
it's nice to be informal.

252
00:17:53,517 --> 00:17:55,917
Especially when a couple is alone.

253
00:17:55,917 --> 00:18:00,197
People like us should lead the
fight to keep tradition going.

254
00:18:00,197 --> 00:18:04,317
If you mean we can never
change, I can't agree. Nor me.

255
00:18:04,317 --> 00:18:08,517
Accepting change is as
important as defending the past.

256
00:18:08,517 --> 00:18:11,957
The role of houses like
Downton is to protect tradition.

257
00:18:11,957 --> 00:18:16,357
That's why they're so important
to maintain. Don't you agree?

258
00:18:16,357 --> 00:18:20,397
We must do everything in our
power to keep Downton going. Sure,

259
00:18:20,397 --> 00:18:24,677
if you think it's worth it.
Who's coming to dinner next week?

260
00:18:26,037 --> 00:18:29,797
Locals. We thought you'd
like to see Downton on parade.

261
00:18:29,797 --> 00:18:32,877
That's right. I'm glad
we've planned a dinner.

262
00:18:32,877 --> 00:18:35,317
We can show you the real point of Downton.

263
00:18:37,277 --> 00:18:39,637
I don't know what to say, Mr Carson.

264
00:18:39,637 --> 00:18:43,917
What's going on here? Alfred
has embarrassed the family.

265
00:18:43,917 --> 00:18:48,157
He forced Mr Matthew to appear
downstairs improperly dressed.

266
00:18:48,157 --> 00:18:52,597
You make it sound quite exciting.
I will not tolerate vulgarity.

267
00:18:52,597 --> 00:18:55,637
I'm sure Alfred didn't mean to. Hm.

268
00:18:57,477 --> 00:19:01,517
I asked Thomas... Hey, what's
this? The stuff you gave me

269
00:19:01,517 --> 00:19:06,237
burned a hole in the tails.
I gave you some soda crystals.

270
00:19:06,237 --> 00:19:08,917
If you use them wrongly,
it's not my fault.

271
00:19:08,917 --> 00:19:13,037
This is what comes of making
him run before he could walk.

272
00:19:18,277 --> 00:19:21,277
If you want me to stay
away from her, I will.

273
00:19:21,277 --> 00:19:23,637
I know it sounds harsh.

274
00:19:23,637 --> 00:19:25,917
Please, I understand completely.

275
00:19:25,917 --> 00:19:29,997
Edith's your daughter, you don't
want her seeing an old cripple.

276
00:19:29,997 --> 00:19:32,277
Now you're the one who's harsh.

277
00:19:33,197 --> 00:19:35,837
The trouble is she calls round regularly.

278
00:19:35,837 --> 00:19:39,197
I can hardly ask her not to be admitted.

279
00:19:39,197 --> 00:19:41,477
I suppose I could write to her.

280
00:19:42,557 --> 00:19:46,477
I hope you won't feel we
can't be friends after this.

281
00:19:46,477 --> 00:19:50,877
Let's leave it for a while. I'll
duck out of the dinner next week.

282
00:19:50,877 --> 00:19:53,557
It might be best. Thank you.

283
00:19:57,037 --> 00:20:00,837
That's the one you should've
taken, soda crystals.

284
00:20:00,837 --> 00:20:05,197
But he didn't give me that, he
pointed to this one. I promise.

285
00:20:06,837 --> 00:20:10,037
You don't have to promise, I believe you.

286
00:20:19,574 --> 00:20:21,974
So you think he's not ready?

287
00:20:22,854 --> 00:20:24,654
He's just a lad, my lord.

288
00:20:24,654 --> 00:20:29,134
He can see to the odd visitor,
but permanent valet to Mr Matthew?

289
00:20:29,134 --> 00:20:33,814
It's too much. I'm pretty sure Mr
Crawley'd rather manage on his own.

290
00:20:33,814 --> 00:20:36,334
They wouldn't like that
downstairs, my lord.

291
00:20:36,334 --> 00:20:38,414
I was afraid you'd say that.

292
00:20:38,414 --> 00:20:40,414
So what would you suggest?

293
00:20:41,574 --> 00:20:44,014
Ask Mr Molesley to join us.

294
00:20:44,014 --> 00:20:46,654
It'd be kinder to Alfred in the long run,

295
00:20:46,654 --> 00:20:49,374
kinder than asking more than he can give.

296
00:20:57,614 --> 00:21:01,814
How is that poor footman? I
thought Carson would eat him alive.

297
00:21:01,814 --> 00:21:04,894
Very glum. To be honest,
he has been a clot.

298
00:21:04,894 --> 00:21:08,854
I'll have to send the coat to
London. Get it done quickly.

299
00:21:08,854 --> 00:21:11,894
This dinner has to be
the grandest of the grand.

300
00:21:11,894 --> 00:21:15,454
What do you hope to show
her? Why Downton matters,

301
00:21:15,454 --> 00:21:17,734
why it mustn't fall apart.

302
00:21:17,734 --> 00:21:20,534
Hasn't Cora had her
share of Levinson gold?

303
00:21:20,534 --> 00:21:22,934
What's left is headed for your uncle.

304
00:21:22,934 --> 00:21:25,054
He's as rich as Croesus as it is.

305
00:21:25,054 --> 00:21:29,534
So you mean to fleece her? Since
you could get us out of this hole,

306
00:21:29,534 --> 00:21:32,174
I won't take any criticism, thank you.

307
00:21:32,174 --> 00:21:36,614
Will she do it? Granny will
make her or die in the attempt.

308
00:21:36,614 --> 00:21:40,014
Now stop talking and kiss
me before I get cross.

309
00:21:43,334 --> 00:21:46,134
You heard him, you'll know at once.

310
00:21:47,454 --> 00:21:50,494
I wish you could get
those maids under control.

311
00:21:50,494 --> 00:21:54,134
They've broken one of the
serving dishes this time.

312
00:21:54,134 --> 00:21:58,334
We're short of a footman, a kitchen
maid and one house maid at least,

313
00:21:58,334 --> 00:22:02,934
that's if Anna's to be a proper
lady's maid, what Lady Mary wants.

314
00:22:02,934 --> 00:22:05,414
Naturally. She likes things done properly.

315
00:22:05,414 --> 00:22:10,454
We can't do things properly until
his lordship allows us the staff

316
00:22:10,454 --> 00:22:13,174
or until you and the blessed Lady Mary

317
00:22:13,174 --> 00:22:16,494
come down from that cloud
and join the human race!

318
00:22:16,494 --> 00:22:20,094
I can only suppose you
are over-tired. Good night.

319
00:22:20,094 --> 00:22:22,454
You see, she... Good night, Mr Carson.

320
00:22:22,454 --> 00:22:25,974
We will discuss the dinner in the morning.

321
00:22:25,974 --> 00:22:27,694
Hm.

322
00:22:29,774 --> 00:22:33,134
And no, Mrs Patmore, you may not tell him.

323
00:22:36,894 --> 00:22:39,614
Oh. Have you finished with Mr Matthew?

324
00:22:39,614 --> 00:22:43,214
I have. He's in the
dining room. You won't need

325
00:22:43,214 --> 00:22:47,254
to attend to him again, Mr Molesley
is coming up from the village.

326
00:22:47,254 --> 00:22:50,254
Has Mr Matthew complained?
It's not your fault,

327
00:22:50,254 --> 00:22:54,334
we've hurried you along too
fast. You mustn't feel badly.

328
00:22:58,134 --> 00:23:02,614
This is Thomas's doing,
but don't you fret,

329
00:23:02,614 --> 00:23:04,734
I'll make him sorry.

330
00:23:26,574 --> 00:23:29,014
I'm on your side.

331
00:23:29,014 --> 00:23:31,414
I'm glad somebody is.

332
00:23:41,334 --> 00:23:46,614
No Mary? She's a married woman now
so she can have breakfast in bed.

333
00:23:46,614 --> 00:23:49,214
Hm. I'm sorry about your tails.

334
00:23:49,214 --> 00:23:53,494
Carson's sending them up on
the London train this morning.

335
00:23:53,494 --> 00:23:55,734
They'll have to put a new panel in.

336
00:23:55,734 --> 00:23:59,974
We thought we'd get Molesley
to come and look after you.

337
00:23:59,974 --> 00:24:02,734
He knows your ways. I'm perfectly happy...

338
00:24:02,734 --> 00:24:07,534
I think it best if he comes. I must
talk to you about the staff we need.

339
00:24:07,534 --> 00:24:12,334
Not now, Carson. Send for Molesley
if Mrs Crawley has no objection.

340
00:24:12,334 --> 00:24:14,334
(SPOON CLATTERS)

341
00:24:16,214 --> 00:24:18,094
Edith?

342
00:24:19,374 --> 00:24:21,374
Oh, Papa, how could you?

343
00:24:23,214 --> 00:24:25,214
(CRIES)

344
00:24:26,534 --> 00:24:29,654
Crawley, do you know what that was?

345
00:24:30,174 --> 00:24:32,254
I'm afraid I probably do.

346
00:24:35,454 --> 00:24:38,734
I'm sorry but enough of my father's money

347
00:24:38,734 --> 00:24:41,294
has already been poured into Downton.

348
00:24:41,294 --> 00:24:45,174
Why should Harold lose half
his inheritance for our folly?

349
00:24:45,174 --> 00:24:48,934
So it's all Papa's fault? It
isn't my mother's or brother's.

350
00:24:48,934 --> 00:24:52,894
I don't see why they should
pay for it. We'll still ask.

351
00:24:52,894 --> 00:24:56,934
What are you so afraid of? If we
sell, we move to a smaller house

352
00:24:56,934 --> 00:25:00,974
and a more modest estate. We
don't have to go down the mine.

353
00:25:00,974 --> 00:25:05,014
You don't understand. Mary, a lot
of people live in smaller houses

354
00:25:05,014 --> 00:25:09,254
than they used to. Which shows
you're American and I'm English.

355
00:25:09,254 --> 00:25:13,294
I shall be Countess of Grantham
one day and the Countess of Grantham

356
00:25:13,294 --> 00:25:15,534
lives at Downton Abbey.

357
00:25:25,294 --> 00:25:27,974
'What job might you be suited to?'

358
00:25:28,374 --> 00:25:33,694
We're not here to give you food, we
want you to find your place in life.

359
00:25:37,294 --> 00:25:39,614
Do you want to speak to me?

360
00:25:40,534 --> 00:25:42,534
Yes, Mrs Crawley, I do.

361
00:25:44,094 --> 00:25:48,134
Have you come for our help?
You're very welcome if you have.

362
00:25:50,494 --> 00:25:52,614
Wait a minute, I know you.

363
00:25:53,654 --> 00:25:57,694
You were the maid who brought your child

364
00:25:57,694 --> 00:26:02,614
into the dining room at Downton.
Sorry, this has been a mistake.

365
00:26:02,614 --> 00:26:06,654
I thought I was ready to ask
you but I'm not. I'm not ready.

366
00:26:06,814 --> 00:26:08,734
Ask me what?

367
00:26:09,214 --> 00:26:11,214
(DOOR OPENS)

368
00:26:14,374 --> 00:26:16,734
I'm sorry to keep you waiting, ladies.

369
00:26:17,854 --> 00:26:19,894
It's not quite as simple.

370
00:26:19,894 --> 00:26:24,814
Oh, my God! Mrs Patmore, will you
please leave the hysteria to me?

371
00:26:25,934 --> 00:26:28,654
I'm afraid the test was inconclusive.

372
00:26:28,654 --> 00:26:33,574
I had hoped the fluid from the cyst
would be clear but there's blood,

373
00:26:33,574 --> 00:26:36,134
not enough to confirm the presence

374
00:26:36,134 --> 00:26:38,654
of cancer but too much to exclude it.

375
00:26:40,094 --> 00:26:42,134
So what happens now?

376
00:26:42,134 --> 00:26:46,254
I send it away for analysis and
this stage will take some time.

377
00:26:46,254 --> 00:26:48,654
How much time? Anything up to two months.

378
00:26:48,654 --> 00:26:50,654
Oh, my...

379
00:26:50,654 --> 00:26:54,254
Until then, try to take it more easy.

380
00:26:54,254 --> 00:26:56,734
Sit down and put your feet up if you can.

381
00:26:56,734 --> 00:26:58,974
Chance'd be a fine thing.

382
00:26:58,974 --> 00:27:02,654
Would you like me to say
something to Lady Grantham?

383
00:27:02,654 --> 00:27:07,294
No, thank you, Doctor, I'll
speak to her myself if I need to.

384
00:27:07,294 --> 00:27:09,174
Thank you.

385
00:27:09,254 --> 00:27:11,254
(CRIES)

386
00:27:14,014 --> 00:27:16,494
My darling girl, what's this?

387
00:27:16,494 --> 00:27:20,014
I think you know, since you
asked Sir Anthony to write.

388
00:27:20,014 --> 00:27:23,654
Edith, you understand I only
want what's best for you.

389
00:27:23,654 --> 00:27:26,654
And you're the judge of that? In this.

390
00:27:26,654 --> 00:27:30,094
Sybil marries a chauffeur
and you welcome him here,

391
00:27:30,094 --> 00:27:35,614
but when I love a gentleman, you
cast him out. She has a point.

392
00:27:35,614 --> 00:27:41,494
Strallan is certainly a gentleman.
Besides which he has a house,

393
00:27:41,494 --> 00:27:46,094
he has money, a title,
everything that you care about.

394
00:27:46,094 --> 00:27:48,534
You make me sound very shallow. Aren't you

395
00:27:48,534 --> 00:27:52,214
when you make me give him
up because he has a bad arm?

396
00:27:52,214 --> 00:27:54,934
He's quarter of a century too old.

397
00:27:54,934 --> 00:27:58,894
Did she tell you that? Your
daughter is sad and lonely.

398
00:27:58,894 --> 00:28:02,374
Now I don't mean to
interfere... Don't you?

399
00:28:02,374 --> 00:28:05,934
If you ban him from Downton
I'll only go to his house.

400
00:28:05,934 --> 00:28:10,134
I don't believe he'd see you.
I'll wait outside until he does.

401
00:28:10,134 --> 00:28:12,734
How can you not like
him because of his age

402
00:28:12,734 --> 00:28:16,014
when almost every man
we grew up with is dead?

403
00:28:16,014 --> 00:28:20,094
Do you want me to spend my life
alone? I do like him very much.

404
00:28:20,734 --> 00:28:23,854
So do I, Papa. So do I.

405
00:28:25,254 --> 00:28:27,854
Please, ask him back.

406
00:28:27,854 --> 00:28:31,654
He writes he's not coming to
Mama's dinner. Please make him.

407
00:28:31,654 --> 00:28:33,814
Please, please, please.

408
00:28:36,454 --> 00:28:38,534
Oh, all right, then.

409
00:28:44,574 --> 00:28:47,174
I found Mrs Bartlett.

410
00:28:47,174 --> 00:28:50,574
I wrote back to the tenant
of the house explaining

411
00:28:50,574 --> 00:28:53,374
and they sent me a forwarding address.

412
00:28:53,374 --> 00:28:55,854
I don't know why they didn't before.

413
00:28:55,854 --> 00:28:59,374
But it doesn't mean she'll
talk to you. Why not?

414
00:28:59,374 --> 00:29:03,374
Audrey Bartlett was the nearest
thing Vera had to a friend.

415
00:29:03,374 --> 00:29:07,174
That's why I want to meet her.
She won't see the real Anna Bates.

416
00:29:07,174 --> 00:29:11,014
She's doesn't have to like
me. I need her to be honest.

417
00:29:11,014 --> 00:29:13,494
I'm going to write and ask for a meeting.

418
00:29:13,494 --> 00:29:15,734
I can get to London and back in a day.

419
00:29:15,734 --> 00:29:17,574
She won't agree.

420
00:29:17,574 --> 00:29:21,734
I've the rent from the house,
I can make it worth her while.

421
00:29:21,734 --> 00:29:23,534
Why do you think Vera

422
00:29:23,534 --> 00:29:27,214
didn't go and see her instead
of sending that letter? Eh?

423
00:29:27,214 --> 00:29:30,174
When Vera was frightened about your visit

424
00:29:30,174 --> 00:29:34,414
she wrote that letter saying how
scared she was instead of seeing her.

425
00:29:35,934 --> 00:29:38,174
Maybe she did both.

426
00:29:40,214 --> 00:29:41,934
So...

427
00:29:41,934 --> 00:29:43,934
what's the news at home?

428
00:29:43,934 --> 00:29:46,534
I shouldn't tell you, really.

429
00:29:47,654 --> 00:29:49,934
I haven't told any of the others.

430
00:29:50,734 --> 00:29:53,614
It's breaking the code of a lady's maid.

431
00:29:54,494 --> 00:29:56,894
His lordship's in trouble.

432
00:29:58,734 --> 00:30:01,374
It seems he may have to sell.

433
00:30:01,374 --> 00:30:04,694
What? Sell Downton?

434
00:30:06,894 --> 00:30:08,894
That makes me sad.

435
00:30:11,174 --> 00:30:15,334
I wouldn't have thought much could
touch me in here, but that does.

436
00:30:18,654 --> 00:30:21,174
(BREATHES HEAVILY)

437
00:30:28,734 --> 00:30:30,774
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

438
00:30:32,654 --> 00:30:36,854
It hasn't come. They promised
and promised and I thought

439
00:30:36,854 --> 00:30:40,454
it was sure to be on the
7 o'clock but it's not.

440
00:30:40,454 --> 00:30:43,174
I'll just have to wear a black tie.

441
00:30:43,174 --> 00:30:45,574
But Lady Mary, surely...

442
00:30:45,574 --> 00:30:49,894
These things don't matter as much
as they did, Lady Mary knows that.

443
00:31:04,014 --> 00:31:07,014
Thank you. I know I'm early...

444
00:31:07,014 --> 00:31:10,254
Granny, come and see what we've done. Oh.

445
00:31:15,214 --> 00:31:17,254
What do you think?

446
00:31:17,734 --> 00:31:20,654
Nothing succeeds like excess.

447
00:31:20,654 --> 00:31:24,294
When shall we tackle her? After dinner.

448
00:31:24,294 --> 00:31:28,294
We'll get her on her own. She
wouldn't want to see all this go,

449
00:31:28,294 --> 00:31:30,374
now it's for her granddaughter.

450
00:31:30,374 --> 00:31:35,014
Never mistake a wish for a
certainty. Let's hope she won't.

451
00:31:38,454 --> 00:31:42,454
Mrs Patmore. What is it
now? It's smoking, the range.

452
00:31:42,454 --> 00:31:45,734
The wind's in the wrong
direction, rake it through.

453
00:31:45,734 --> 00:31:48,694
Where's Alfred?! In the servants' hall.

454
00:31:51,094 --> 00:31:55,294
Where are they? His what?
His bloody evening shirts!

455
00:31:56,494 --> 00:32:00,614
Where have you put them? I
haven't touched them. Why would I?

456
00:32:02,494 --> 00:32:07,094
Have you done this? Why would I
know anything about his shirts?

457
00:32:08,094 --> 00:32:12,294
When I find out... Keep your
histrionics to yourself. Hurry up.

458
00:32:12,294 --> 00:32:16,694
Her ladyship's in the drawing room.
His lordship's not even dressed?

459
00:32:21,934 --> 00:32:25,974
You can't have lost them all.
I haven't lost any of them.

460
00:32:25,974 --> 00:32:28,894
They've been taken by someone, stolen.

461
00:32:28,894 --> 00:32:31,134
Why would they do that? To get at me.

462
00:32:33,654 --> 00:32:35,974
Are you not popular downstairs?

463
00:32:35,974 --> 00:32:38,174
I wouldn't say that, my lord,

464
00:32:39,454 --> 00:32:41,734
but you know how people can be.

465
00:32:41,734 --> 00:32:45,654
They like a little joke.
This is quite unacceptable.

466
00:32:45,654 --> 00:32:48,414
If you uncover the
culprit, refer them to me,

467
00:32:48,414 --> 00:32:50,774
but for now what are we going to do?

468
00:33:07,894 --> 00:33:09,974
Good evening, Sir John.

469
00:33:15,134 --> 00:33:18,854
It can't be going out. There
must be a block in the flue.

470
00:33:18,854 --> 00:33:22,734
The dinner's not cooked, we
haven't put in the souffles.

471
00:33:22,734 --> 00:33:26,734
There'll be no souffles tonight.
What on earth is going on?

472
00:33:26,734 --> 00:33:30,294
We've 20 lords and
ladies in the drawing room

473
00:33:30,294 --> 00:33:35,094
waiting for dinner and we've got
no dinner to give them. Oh, my God.

474
00:33:45,508 --> 00:33:47,628
Why are you not in white tie?

475
00:33:47,628 --> 00:33:50,108
I'm afraid they never sent my tails back.

476
00:33:50,108 --> 00:33:54,588
You are not in white tie,
either. What have you come as?

477
00:33:54,588 --> 00:33:58,948
I'm sorry, Thomas has lost my
dress shirts. Why is he still here?

478
00:33:58,948 --> 00:34:02,868
I thought you gave him
his marching orders. I had.

479
00:34:02,868 --> 00:34:07,108
My dear mother-in-law intervened.
I've a good mind to tell her...

480
00:34:07,108 --> 00:34:10,428
No, she must have it
all her own way tonight.

481
00:34:10,428 --> 00:34:14,468
You two are dressed for a barbecue.
I feel like a Chicago bootlegger.

482
00:34:14,468 --> 00:34:17,988
That sounds almost as
peculiar as you look.

483
00:34:17,988 --> 00:34:21,788
Come quickly. What is it?
The oven's broken down.

484
00:34:21,788 --> 00:34:24,148
It can't have. What does that mean?

485
00:34:24,148 --> 00:34:27,508
It means we have no food. Oh.

486
00:34:27,508 --> 00:34:31,028
Funny clothes and no food.
Should be quite an evening.

487
00:34:31,028 --> 00:34:33,028
Thank you, Mother.

488
00:34:36,108 --> 00:34:39,988
Nothing's cooked and
nothing's going to be cooked.

489
00:34:39,988 --> 00:34:42,708
But surely... Shall we
tell them to go home?

490
00:34:42,708 --> 00:34:46,468
No, they've come for a party,
we'll give them a party.

491
00:34:46,468 --> 00:34:49,388
Clear the table, go down to the larders,

492
00:34:49,388 --> 00:34:52,748
bring up bread, fruit,
cheese, chicken, ham.

493
00:34:52,748 --> 00:34:55,228
We're going to have an indoor picnic.

494
00:34:55,228 --> 00:34:59,148
They're going to eat whatever
they want, wherever they want.

495
00:34:59,148 --> 00:35:02,428
Are you quite sure, madam?
It's not how we do it.

496
00:35:02,428 --> 00:35:06,548
How you used to do it.
It might be fun. I agree.

497
00:35:06,548 --> 00:35:09,828
We'll all pull together
and it'll be great fun. Yes!

498
00:35:09,828 --> 00:35:13,308
I know what we need. Does
anyone play the piano?

499
00:35:13,308 --> 00:35:16,068
This is so not what we wanted it to be.

500
00:35:16,068 --> 00:35:20,508
If it's the end of your undignified
campaign, I won't be sorry.

501
00:35:21,628 --> 00:35:25,228
We can't just give up. Certainly not.

502
00:35:26,068 --> 00:35:28,628
Do you think I might have a drink?

503
00:35:28,628 --> 00:35:32,148
Oh, I'm so sorry, I
thought you were a waiter.

504
00:35:33,868 --> 00:35:38,828
Slice that finely, fetch some
parsley, cut the dry bits off.

505
00:35:38,828 --> 00:35:41,228
Can I lend a hand? I don't mind helping.

506
00:35:41,228 --> 00:35:45,268
I think it's good to do other
things sometimes. I know you do.

507
00:35:45,268 --> 00:35:49,428
There's not much left of this.
Cut it in squares, put it with ham.

508
00:35:49,428 --> 00:35:52,228
You're very smart in your valet's outfit.

509
00:35:52,228 --> 00:35:54,668
Alfred, check the meat larder,

510
00:35:54,668 --> 00:35:58,788
bring anything back you think
a human being could swallow.

511
00:36:01,028 --> 00:36:05,068
Chop, chop, Mrs Hughes. We can
rest later but not yet. Mr Carson...

512
00:36:05,068 --> 00:36:08,588
Mr Carson's right, there's
not a minute to lose.

513
00:36:14,908 --> 00:36:17,588
(SNIFFS) Psst!

514
00:36:18,508 --> 00:36:22,588
Do you want to know a secret? The
shirts Thomas thinks he stole...

515
00:36:22,588 --> 00:36:25,668
I saw who took them and
I know where they are.

516
00:36:25,668 --> 00:36:28,428
Who did take them? Never mind that,

517
00:36:28,428 --> 00:36:32,308
but I followed. I'll show you if you want.

518
00:36:32,308 --> 00:36:35,108
Why are you being so nice to me?

519
00:36:35,108 --> 00:36:39,708
Because I like you. And you
can say it, just like that?

520
00:36:39,708 --> 00:36:42,988
I'm an American, Alfred, and this is 1920.

521
00:36:44,028 --> 00:36:46,068
Time to live a little.

522
00:36:46,068 --> 00:36:50,868
I thought you was trying to find
something about for Mrs Levinson.

523
00:36:50,868 --> 00:36:55,588
Find out what when she reads
them like the palm of her hand?

524
00:36:55,588 --> 00:36:58,748
She won't help, you know? Help with what?

525
00:36:58,748 --> 00:37:02,348
Never mind. Just kiss me again.

526
00:37:08,028 --> 00:37:12,228
Now, all of you find whatever
it is that you want to eat

527
00:37:12,228 --> 00:37:14,708
and take it wherever you want to sit.

528
00:37:14,708 --> 00:37:16,668
Anywhere? Anywhere.

529
00:37:16,668 --> 00:37:21,868
If you have ever wanted to explore
Downton, this is your chance.

530
00:37:21,868 --> 00:37:25,348
I'm sorry if it's all a
bit casual. It's exciting.

531
00:37:25,348 --> 00:37:29,308
I feel like the bright young
people they write about.

532
00:37:29,308 --> 00:37:33,108
Thank you, Lady Mellendorf. Cheer
up, she won't be here forever.

533
00:37:33,108 --> 00:37:36,268
How much damage will
be done before she goes?

534
00:37:36,268 --> 00:37:40,708
♪ Let me call you "Sweetheart"

535
00:37:40,708 --> 00:37:46,468
♪ I'm in love with you

536
00:37:47,428 --> 00:37:51,348
♪ Let me hear you whisper

537
00:37:51,348 --> 00:37:56,868
♪ That you love me too

538
00:37:58,668 --> 00:38:04,028
♪ Keep the love-light glowing

539
00:38:04,028 --> 00:38:08,828
♪ In your eyes so true

540
00:38:09,948 --> 00:38:14,428
♪ Let me call you "Sweetheart"

541
00:38:14,748 --> 00:38:19,028
♪ I'm in love

542
00:38:20,068 --> 00:38:23,548
♪ With

543
00:38:23,548 --> 00:38:25,468
♪ You ♪

544
00:38:30,788 --> 00:38:33,668
Is there anything for our supper?

545
00:38:34,148 --> 00:38:36,268
I've taken a veal and egg pie.

546
00:38:37,468 --> 00:38:40,948
Oh, I wish you'd let me talk to Mr Carson.

547
00:38:40,948 --> 00:38:44,988
I don't want to be a sick woman in
his eyes for the next two months,

548
00:38:45,868 --> 00:38:49,228
or a dying one in the
months to come after that.

549
00:38:50,188 --> 00:38:52,788
(CRIES)

550
00:38:52,788 --> 00:38:55,868
Shh. I know it'll be all right.

551
00:38:59,028 --> 00:39:00,868
No, you don't,

552
00:39:00,868 --> 00:39:03,228
but I appreciate the sentiment.

553
00:39:04,428 --> 00:39:07,348
Are you sure you won't
wake up in ten years

554
00:39:07,348 --> 00:39:10,748
and wonder why you're tied
to this crippled codger?

555
00:39:10,748 --> 00:39:13,348
Only if you keep talking like that.

556
00:39:13,348 --> 00:39:15,988
Do you know how much you mean to me?

557
00:39:17,108 --> 00:39:19,388
You have given me back my life.

558
00:39:19,388 --> 00:39:21,228
That's more like it.

559
00:39:27,548 --> 00:39:31,588
You won't wait? To give you
the chance to change your mind?

560
00:39:31,588 --> 00:39:35,388
Don't worry, I can get
it organised in a month.

561
00:39:35,388 --> 00:39:37,948
Shall we tell them tonight?

562
00:39:37,948 --> 00:39:41,068
No, no, I'll come back in the morning.

563
00:39:46,548 --> 00:39:50,548
Can I ask you something? Why
do you like that American girl?

564
00:39:50,548 --> 00:39:53,348
Steady. Who says I do? Don't you?

565
00:39:53,348 --> 00:39:57,428
I suppose I do. And it
doesn't matter that she's fast

566
00:39:57,428 --> 00:40:01,028
or that you won't see her
again after she's gone home?

567
00:40:01,028 --> 00:40:06,068
She made me feel good about myself
for the first time here. Alfred!

568
00:40:06,068 --> 00:40:09,708
Hurry up, I need you to
take around the claret.

569
00:40:09,708 --> 00:40:12,108
Won't they spill it on the floor?

570
00:40:12,108 --> 00:40:16,228
If you ask me, we are staring
into the chaos of Gomorrah,

571
00:40:16,228 --> 00:40:20,548
but we have to give them more
wine and you are going to help.

572
00:40:20,548 --> 00:40:24,548
What's that? I have to take
it upstairs for his lordship.

573
00:40:24,548 --> 00:40:26,588
Well, be quick about it.

574
00:40:28,468 --> 00:40:32,308
I'll help you any way
I can. Thank heavens.

575
00:40:32,308 --> 00:40:36,188
It seems our family
owes Downton's survival

576
00:40:36,188 --> 00:40:39,428
to the Levinsons not once but twice.

577
00:40:39,428 --> 00:40:43,588
No. I'm so sorry, but
you've misunderstood me.

578
00:40:43,588 --> 00:40:47,988
I cannot rescue Downton.
It's a shame if it has to go,

579
00:40:47,988 --> 00:40:51,188
but I can't. But why not?

580
00:40:51,188 --> 00:40:54,148
Because your grandpa tied the money down.

581
00:40:54,148 --> 00:40:57,308
He felt the Crawley
family had quite enough.

582
00:40:57,308 --> 00:40:59,628
But you said you'd help us.

583
00:40:59,628 --> 00:41:04,188
I can entertain all of you
in Newport and in New York

584
00:41:04,188 --> 00:41:08,348
and I can add to Cora's dress
allowance, but that's all.

585
00:41:08,348 --> 00:41:11,788
My income is generous but
I cannot touch the capital.

586
00:41:11,788 --> 00:41:15,628
Besides, Mary, the world has changed.

587
00:41:15,628 --> 00:41:18,748
These houses were built for another age.

588
00:41:18,748 --> 00:41:23,828
Are you sure you want to continue
with the bother of it all?

589
00:41:23,828 --> 00:41:29,188
Quite sure. If I knew I was going to
lose it, I'd look on the sunny side.

590
00:41:30,868 --> 00:41:35,828
Both of our husbands tied the money
up tight before they were taken.

591
00:41:37,228 --> 00:41:39,788
Lord Grantham wasn't taken.

592
00:41:40,788 --> 00:41:42,588
He died.

593
00:41:57,028 --> 00:42:00,308
I suppose it's scraps
for the servants tonight?

594
00:42:00,308 --> 00:42:02,428
Mrs Patmore's kept something by.

595
00:42:03,348 --> 00:42:05,428
Who put them back?

596
00:42:05,908 --> 00:42:09,748
What? The shirts, who put them back?

597
00:42:09,748 --> 00:42:13,668
They're back? You mean you
overlooked them in the first place.

598
00:42:13,668 --> 00:42:15,708
Don't tell me what I mean.

599
00:42:15,708 --> 00:42:21,828
I'm warning you. You sound like
Tom Mix in a Wild-West picture show!

600
00:42:21,828 --> 00:42:25,868
Stop warning me and go lay
out his lordship's pyjamas.

601
00:42:25,868 --> 00:42:28,188
(TITTERS)

602
00:42:28,188 --> 00:42:32,468
What are you laughing at? Those
missing shirts went for a walk

603
00:42:32,468 --> 00:42:35,628
and now they've come
home. Really? Have they?

604
00:42:44,108 --> 00:42:46,868
You didn't see nothing. I agree.

605
00:42:47,948 --> 00:42:50,508
Cos if you did I'll cut ya.

606
00:43:00,428 --> 00:43:02,148
Oh!

607
00:43:07,708 --> 00:43:09,788
Don't ever threaten me.

608
00:43:11,868 --> 00:43:15,668
I forgot I was sharing
a cell with a murderer.

609
00:43:17,228 --> 00:43:19,228
Don't forget it again.

610
00:43:32,308 --> 00:43:35,588
This evening has made
me homesick for America.

611
00:43:36,388 --> 00:43:38,388
It's time to go.

612
00:43:39,868 --> 00:43:43,068
I don't suppose you want
some whisky to take to bed?

613
00:43:43,068 --> 00:43:46,228
Oh, but I'd love one. No water.

614
00:43:52,628 --> 00:43:54,508
Thank you.

615
00:43:54,508 --> 00:43:58,228
I'm sorry I can't help
you keep Downton, Robert.

616
00:44:02,668 --> 00:44:04,748
That's what Mary wanted.

617
00:44:04,748 --> 00:44:06,468
Ah.

618
00:44:07,308 --> 00:44:09,348
I thought there was something.

619
00:44:09,348 --> 00:44:12,508
You know, the way to
deal with the world today

620
00:44:12,508 --> 00:44:16,548
is not to ignore it. If
you do you'll just get hurt.

621
00:44:16,548 --> 00:44:19,628
Sometimes, I feel like
a creature in the wilds

622
00:44:19,628 --> 00:44:23,188
whose natural habitat is
gradually being destroyed.

623
00:44:23,188 --> 00:44:26,788
Some animals adapt to new surroundings.

624
00:44:26,788 --> 00:44:29,348
It seems a better choice than extinction.

625
00:44:30,828 --> 00:44:32,988
I don't think it is a choice.

626
00:44:32,988 --> 00:44:35,308
I think it's what's in you.

627
00:44:36,228 --> 00:44:40,268
Let's hope that what's in you
will carry you through these times

628
00:44:40,268 --> 00:44:42,188
to a safer shore.

629
00:44:56,108 --> 00:44:58,428
Is everything all right?

630
00:44:58,428 --> 00:45:00,188
Certainly.

631
00:45:01,188 --> 00:45:03,868
Was there something you wanted?

632
00:45:05,388 --> 00:45:09,588
The kitchen managed well tonight
in difficult circumstances.

633
00:45:09,588 --> 00:45:11,908
His lordship sent his thanks.

634
00:45:13,028 --> 00:45:15,268
Was the evening a success?

635
00:45:15,268 --> 00:45:18,348
The odd thing is I think it was.

636
00:45:18,348 --> 00:45:21,388
For me, everyone sprawled on the floor

637
00:45:21,388 --> 00:45:26,108
eating like beaters at a break
in the shooting isn't a party,

638
00:45:26,108 --> 00:45:28,148
it's a works outing.

639
00:45:28,148 --> 00:45:31,468
Where's the style, Mrs
Hughes? Where's the show?

640
00:45:31,468 --> 00:45:34,948
Perhaps people are
tired of style and show.

641
00:45:34,948 --> 00:45:39,068
Well, in my opinion,
to misquote Dr Johnson,

642
00:45:39,068 --> 00:45:42,268
if you're tired of style,
you are tired of life.

643
00:45:42,268 --> 00:45:45,068
Hm. Good night, Mr Carson.

644
00:45:46,748 --> 00:45:50,028
You'd say if anything
was wrong, wouldn't you?

645
00:45:51,148 --> 00:45:55,548
I know I've been a bit
crabby, but I am on your side.

646
00:45:57,108 --> 00:45:58,948
Thank you for that.

647
00:46:15,828 --> 00:46:21,268
You've just missed an admirer. Mr
Carson says you did well tonight.

648
00:46:21,268 --> 00:46:23,948
Oh. Did you tell him?

649
00:46:24,788 --> 00:46:26,508
No.

650
00:46:27,588 --> 00:46:29,708
What is there to tell?

651
00:46:30,628 --> 00:46:32,868
One day I will die.

652
00:46:33,788 --> 00:46:35,828
And so will he. And you.

653
00:46:35,828 --> 00:46:38,388
And every one of us under this roof.

654
00:46:39,148 --> 00:46:41,188
(SIGHS)

655
00:46:41,188 --> 00:46:44,988
You must put these things
in proportion, Mrs Patmore,

656
00:46:45,828 --> 00:46:47,948
and I think I can do that now.

657
00:47:01,548 --> 00:47:04,828
'Something happening in
this house is about me.'

658
00:47:04,828 --> 00:47:07,828
'Strallan is the traditional choice.'

659
00:47:07,828 --> 00:47:10,988
Edith is beginning her
life as an old man's drudge.

660
00:47:10,988 --> 00:47:15,028
This is the moment you receive
a huge fortune to save Downton

661
00:47:15,028 --> 00:47:19,028
and you give it away. I hope
you can understand... I'm trying.

662
00:47:19,028 --> 00:47:23,068
Downton Place. Where am I to
go? We still own the village.

663
00:47:23,068 --> 00:47:26,228
I could open a shop.
Fashionably late is one thing.

664
00:47:26,228 --> 00:47:28,748
So it is Cancer? Don't say anything,

665
00:47:28,748 --> 00:47:31,508
'she'd hate to think the doctor told you.'

666
00:47:32,388 --> 00:47:34,388
itfc subtitles

